\" class=\"ltx_tr\">\n367\t<td id=\"S2.T1.2.1.7.5.1\" class=\"ltx_td ltx_align_left\" style=\"padding:-0.5pt 2.0pt;\">SAFMN <cite class=\"ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite\">[<a href=\"#bib.bib21\" title=\"Spatially-adaptive feature modulation for efficient image super-resolution\" class=\"ltx_ref\">21</a>]</cite>\n368\t</td>\n369\t<td id=\"S2.T1.2.1.7.5.2\" class=\"ltx_td ltx_align_justify\" style=\"padding:-0.5pt 2.0pt;\">\n370\t<span id=\"S2.T1.2.1.7.5.2.1\" class=\"ltx_inline-block ltx_align_top\">\n371\t<span id=\"S2.T1.2.1.7.5.2.1.1\" class=\"ltx_p ltx_align_left\">Feature-mixing network with spatially-adaptive modulation.</span>\n372\t</span>\n373\t</td>\n374\t<td id=\"S2.T1.2.1.7.5.3\" class=\"ltx_td ltx_align_right\" style=\"padding:-0.5pt 2.0pt;\">+</td>\n375\t<td id=\"S2.T1.2.1.7.5.4\" class=\"ltx_td ltx_nopad_l ltx_align_right\" style=\"padding:-0.5pt 2.0pt;\">0.772</td>\n376\t<td id=\"S2.T1.2.1.7.5.5\" class=\"ltx_td ltx_nopad_l ltx_align_left\" style=\"padding:-0.5pt 2.0pt;\">M</td>\n377\t<td id=\"S2.T1.2.1.7.5.6\" class=\"ltx_td ltx_align_center\" style=\"padding:-0.5pt 2.0pt;\">0.83</td>\n378\t<td id=\"S2.T1.2.1.7.5.7\" class=\"ltx_td ltx_align_center\" style=\"padding:-0.5pt 2.0pt;\">\n379\t<span id=\"S2.T1.2.1.7.5.7.1\" class=\"ltx_ERROR undefined\">\\qty</span>0.52</td>\n380\t<td id=\"S2.T1.2.1.7.5.8\" class=\"ltx_td ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_center\" style=\"padding:-0.5pt 2.0pt;\">\n381\t<span id=\"S2.T1.2.1.7.5.8.1\" class=\"ltx_ERROR undefined\">\\qty</span>1808<span id=\"S2.T1.2.1.7.5.8.2\" class=\"ltx_ERROR undefined\">\\mega</span>\n382\t</td>\n383\t</tr>\n384\t<tr id=\"S2.T1.2.1.8.6\" class=\"ltx_tr\">\n385\t<td id=\"S2.T1.2.1.8.6.1\" class=\"ltx_td ltx_align_left\" style=\"padding:-0.5pt 2.0pt;\">AINN <cite class=\"ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite\">[<a href=\"#bib.bib22\" title=\"A circular microphone array with virtual microphones based on acoustics-informed neural networks\" class=\"ltx_ref\">24</a>]</cite>\n386\t</td>\n387\t<td id=\"S2.T1.2.1.8.6.2\" class=\"ltx_td ltx_align_justify\" style=\"padding:-0.5pt 2.0pt;\">\n388\t<span id=\"S2.T1.2.1.8.6.2.1\" class=\"ltx_inline-block ltx_align_top\">\n389\t<span id=\"S2.T1.2.1.8.6.2.1.1\" class=\"ltx_p ltx_align_left\">Acoustics-informed MLP constrained by array geometry.</span>\n390\t</span>\n391\t</td>\n392\t<td id=\"S2.T1.2.1.8.6.3\" class=\"ltx_td ltx_align_right\" style=\"padding:-0.5pt 2.0pt;\">+</td>\n393\t<td id=\"S2.T1.2.1.8.6.4\" class=\"ltx_td ltx_nopad_l ltx_align_right\" style=\"padding:-0.5pt 2.0pt;\">0.007</td>\n394\t<td id=\"S2.T1.2.1.8.6.5\" class=\"ltx_td ltx_nopad_l ltx_align_left\" style=\"padding:-0.5pt 2.0pt;\">M</td>\n395\t<td id=\"S2.T1.2.1.8.6.6\" class=\"ltx_td ltx_align_center\" style=\"padding:-0.5pt 2.0pt;\">0.74</td>\n396\t<td id=\"S2.T1.2.1.8.6.7\" class=\"ltx_td ltx_align_center\" style=\"padding:-0.5pt 2.0pt;\">\n397\t<span id=\"S2.T1.2.1.8.6.7.1\" class=\"ltx_ERROR undefined\">\\qty</span>0.57</td>\n398\t<td id=\"S2.T1.2.1.8.6.8\" class=\"ltx_td ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_center\" style=\"padding:-0.5pt 2.0pt;\">\n399\t<span id=\"S2.T1.2.1.8.6.8.1\" class=\"ltx_ERROR undefined\">\\qty</span>1876<span id=\"S2.T1.2.1.8.6.8.2\" class=\"ltx_ERROR undefined\">\\mega</span>\n400\t</td>\n401\t</tr>\n402\t<tr id=\"S2.T1.2.1.9.7\" class=\"ltx_tr\">\n403\t<td id=\"S2.T1.2.1.9.7.1\" class=\"ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_b\" style=\"padding:-0.5pt 2.0pt;\">GAN <cite class=\"ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite\">[<a href=\"#bib.bib23\" title=\"HRTF upsampling with a GAN using a gnomonic equiangular projection\" class=\"ltx_ref\">8</a>]</cite>\n404\t</td>\n405\t<td id=\"S2.T1.2.1.9.7.2\" class=\"ltx_td ltx_align_justify ltx_border_b\" style=\"padding:-0.5pt 2.0pt;\">\n406\t<span id=\"S2.T1.2.1.9.7.2.1\" class=\"ltx_inline-block ltx_align_top\">\n407\t<span id=\"S2.T1.2.1.9.7.2.1.1\" class=\"ltx_p ltx_align_left\">Adversarial generator to upsample HRTF measurement locations.</span>\n408\t</span>\n409\t</td>\n410\t<td id=\"S2.T1.2.1.9.7.3\" class=\"ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_border_b\" style=\"padding:-0.5pt 2.0pt;\">+</td>\n411\t<td id=\"S2.T1.2.1.9.7.4\" class=\"ltx_td ltx_nopad_l ltx_align_right ltx_border_b\" style=\"padding:-0.5pt 2.0pt;\">4.287</td>\n412\t<td id=\"S2.T1.2.1.9.7.5\" class=\"ltx_td ltx_nopad_l ltx_align_left ltx_border_b\" style=\"padding:-0.5pt 2.0pt;\">M</td>\n413\t<td id=\"S2.T1.2.1.9.7.6\" class=\"ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_b\" style=\"padding:-0.5pt 2.0pt;\">4.95</td>\n414\t<td id=\"S2.T1.2.1.9.7.7\" class=\"ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_b\" style=\"padding:-0.5pt 2.0pt;\">\n415\t<span id=\"S2.T1.2.1.9.7.7.1\" class=\"ltx_ERROR undefined\">\\qty</span>0.61</td>\n416\t<td id=\"S2.T1.2.1.9.7.8\" class=\"ltx_td ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_center ltx_border_b\" style=\"padding:-0.5pt 2.0pt;\">\n417\t<span id=\"S2.T1.2.1.9.7.8.1\" class=\"ltx_ERROR undefined\">\\qty</span>3443<span id=\"S2.T1.2.1.9.7.8.2\" class=\"ltx_ERROR undefined\">\\mega</span>\n418\t</td>\n419\t</tr>\n420\t</tbody>\n421\t</table>\n422\t</span></div>\n423\t<figcaption class=\"ltx_caption\"><span class=\"ltx_tag ltx_tag_table\"><span id=\"S2.T1.3.1.1\" class=\"ltx_text ltx_font_bold\">Table 1</span>: </span>The benchmarked upsampler models, including the parameter count that each adds to LAM (for the GAN, discriminator parameters are excluded as it is not used in inference). GFLOPs, latency and peak inference memory are reported for each including LAM. Peak inference memory is the median peak CUDA memory (see <a href=\"#S3\" title=\"3 Methodology ‣ MIND THE MICROPHONE GAP: BENCHMARKING ARRAY UPSAMPLING STRATEGIES FOR LATENT ACOUSTIC MAPPING\" class=\"ltx_ref\"><span class=\"ltx_text ltx_ref_tag\">Section</span> <span class=\"ltx_text ltx_ref_tag\">3</span></a>).\n424\t</figcaption>\n425\t</figure>\n426\t<div id=\"S2.p2\" class=\"ltx_para ltx_noindent\">\n427\t<p id=\"S2.p2.1\" class=\"ltx_p\"><span id=\"S2.p2.1.1\" class=\"ltx_text ltx_font_bold\">SRCNN:</span> The Super-Resolution Convolutional Neural Network (SRCNN) <cite class=\"ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite\">[<a href=\"#bib.bib18\" title=\"Image super-resolution using deep convolutional networks\" class=\"ltx_ref\">4</a>]</cite> is considered the first of its kind for image super-resolution.\n428\tIt first upsamples the input to the target resolution via bicubic interpolation, then refines the result through three successive operations: overlapping patch extraction, non-linear feature mapping, and reconstruction <cite class=\"ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite\">[<a href=\"#bib.bib18\" title=\"Image super-resolution using deep convolutional networks\" class=\"ltx_ref\">4</a>]</cite>.</p>\n429\t</div>\n430\t<div id=\"S2.p3\" class=\"ltx_para ltx_noindent\">\n431\t<p id=\"S2.p3.1\" class=\"ltx_p\"><span id=\"S2.p3.1.1\" class=\"ltx_text ltx_font_bold\">DBPN:</span> The Deep Back-Projection Network (DBPN) <cite class=\"ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite\">[<a href=\"#bib.bib19\" title=\"Deep back-projection networks for super-resolution\" class=\"ltx_ref\">6</a>]</cite> consists of convolutional up- and down-projection blocks that alternate. Residual connections carry information across all preceding blocks of the same type.\n432\tThe final high-resolution output is produced by concatenating the feature maps from every up-projection stage, allowing the network to exploit multi-depth representations of the input.\n433\tDBPN was used as the sole upsampler in the original LAM <cite class=\"ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite\">[<a href=\"#bib.bib1\" title=\"Latent acoustic mapping for direction of arrival estimation: A self-supervised approach\" class=\"ltx_ref\">18</a>]</cite>.</p>\n434\t</div>\n435\t<div id=\"S2.p4\" class=\"ltx_para ltx_noindent\">\n436\t<p id=\"S2.p4.1\" class=\"ltx_p\"><span id=\"S2.p4.1.1\" class=\"ltx_text ltx_font_bold\">IMDN:</span> The Information Multi-Distillation Network (IMDN) <cite class=\"ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite\">[<a href=\"#bib.bib20\" title=\"Lightweight image super-resolution with information multi-distillation network\" class=\"ltx_ref\">9</a>]</cite> reduces computation via information multi-distillation blocks, each of which progressively distils features and weighs their contribution via contrast-aware attention.\n437\tResidual connections link the blocks, and a sub-pixel convolution performs the final spatial upsampling, yielding a compact model with a smaller parameter count than DBPN.</p>\n438\t</div>\n439\t<div id=\"S2.p5\" class=\"ltx_para ltx_noindent\">\n440\t<p id=\"S2.p5.1\" class=\"ltx_p\"><span id=\"S2.p5.1.1\" class=\"ltx_text ltx_font_bold\">SAFMN:</span> The Spatially-Adaptive Feature Modulation Network (SAFMN) <cite class=\"ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite\">[<a href=\"#bib.bib21\" title=\"Spatially-adaptive feature modulation for efficient image super-resolution\" class=\"ltx_ref\">21</a>]</cite> is a lightweight model centred on global-local feature interaction.\n441\tAt its core is a feature mixing module consisting of a spatially-adaptive modulation layer (depth-wise convolution followed by nearest-neighbour interpolation) and a convolutional channel mixer.</p>\n442\t</div>\n443\t<div id=\"S2.p6\" class=\"ltx_para ltx_noindent\">\n444\t<p id=\"S2.p6.1\" class=\"ltx_p\"><span id=\"S2.p6.1.1\" class=\"ltx_text ltx_font_bold\">AINN:</span> The Acoustics-Informed Neural Network (AINN) <cite class=\"ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite\">[<a href=\"#bib.bib22\" title=\"A circular microphone array with virtual microphones based on acoustics-informed neural networks\" class=\"ltx_ref\">24</a>]</cite> departs from the convolutional paradigm and is instead a multi-layer perceptron. Physics knowledge is injected during training by penalising violations of the Helmholtz equation evaluated on the microphone array’s geometry. Given the CSM’s well-defined acoustic interpretation, the AINN stands out as a domain-specific encoder in our benchmark.</p>\n445\t</div>\n446\t<div id=\"S2.p7\" class=\"ltx_para ltx_noindent\">\n447\t<p id=\"S2.p7.1\" class=\"ltx_p\"><span id=\"S2.p7.1.1\" class=\"ltx_text ltx_font_bold\">GAN Generator:</span> The generator of a Generative Adversarial Network (GAN) can perform super-resolution for head-related transfer functions (HRTFs) <cite class=\"ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite\">[<a href=\"#bib.bib23\" title=\"HRTF upsampling with a GAN using a gnomonic equiangular projection\" class=\"ltx_ref\">8</a>]</cite>.\n448\tThe architecture consists of convolutional blocks that produce the final high-resolution output.\n449\tAdversarial training encourages the generation of more perceptually consistent representations, albeit at the cost of increased training complexity.</p>\n450\t</div>\n451\t<div id=\"S2.p8\" class=\"ltx_para ltx_noindent\">\n452\t<p id=\"S2.p8.1\" class=\"ltx_p\"><span id=\"S2.p8.1.1\" class=\"ltx_text ltx_font_bold\">Bicubic:</span> Used as a low-compute performance-floor reference point.\n453\tIt estimates each output sample from a weighted combination of a <math id=\"S2.p8.1.m1\" class=\"ltx_Math\" alttext=\"4\\times 4\" display=\"inline\" intent=\":literal\"><semantics><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace=\"0.222em\" rspace=\"0.222em\">×</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">4\\times 4</annotation></semantics></math> neighbourhood, yielding smoother transitions than bilinear or nearest-neighbour methods \n454\t<cite class=\"ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite\">[<a href=\"#bib.bib24\" title=\"Cubic convolution interpolation for digital image processing\" class=\"ltx_ref\">12</a>]</cite>.</p>\n455\t</div>\n456\t</section>\n457\t<section id=\"S3\" class=\"ltx_section\">\n458\t<h2 class=\"ltx_title ltx_title_section\">\n459\t<span class=\"ltx_tag ltx_tag_section\">3 </span>Methodology</h2>\n460\t\n461\t<div id=\"S3.p1\" class=\"ltx_para\">\n462\t<p id=\"S3.p1.1\" class=\"ltx_p\">All models contain an upsampler, which serves as the primary experimental factor in our study, followed by LAM <cite class=\"ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite\">[<a href=\"#bib.bib1\" title=\"Latent acoustic mapping for direction of arrival estimation: A self-supervised approach\" class=\"ltx_ref\">18</a>]</cite>.\n463\tIn every experiment, we initialise LAM with the weights released with the original publication.\n464\tThe upsamplers, in contrast, are adapted for our complex-valued CSM task and trained from scratch.\n465\tBelow, we outline the data representations we use and the optimisation procedure for each model.<span id=\"footnote1\" class=\"ltx_note ltx_role_footnote\"><sup class=\"ltx_note_mark\">1</sup><span class=\"ltx_note_outer\"><span class=\"ltx_note_content\"><sup class=\"ltx_note_mark\">1</sup><span class=\"ltx_tag ltx_tag_note\">1</span>Explore the full source code: <a href=\"2607.24463v1/doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.19735049\" title=\"\" class=\"ltx_ref ltx_href\">https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.19735049</a></span></span></span></p>\n466\t</div>\n467\t<section id=\"S3.SS1\" class=\"ltx_subsection\">\n468\t<h3 class=\"ltx_title ltx_title_subsection\">\n469\t<span class=\"ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection\">3.1 </span>Datasets</h3>\n470\t\n471\t<div id=\"S3.SS1.p1\" class=\"ltx_para\">\n472\t<p id=\"S3.SS1.p1.2\" class=\"ltx_p\">To train, we use two types of high-resolution data: synthetic soundscapes that we generate using AudibleLight <cite class=\"ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite\">[<a href=\"#bib.bib25\" title=\"AudibleLight (RC): a controllable, end-to-end API for soundscape synthesis across ray-traced &amp; real-world measured acoustics\" class=\"ltx_ref\">3</a>]</cite>, and real-world recordings from EigenScape <cite class=\"ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite\">[<a href=\"#bib.bib26\" title=\"EigenScape: a database of spatial acoustic scene recordings\" class=\"ltx_ref\">5</a>]</cite>.\n473\tThe training/validation set is partitioned with ratios of <math id=\"S3.SS1.p1.1.m1\" class=\"ltx_Math\" alttext=\"0.89/0.11\" display=\"inline\" intent=\":literal\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.89</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>0.11</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.89/0.11</annotation></semantics></math> for AudibleLight and <math id=\"S3.SS1.p1.2.m2\" class=\"ltx_Math\" alttext=\"0.86/0.14\" display=\"inline\" intent=\":literal\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.86</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>0.14</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.86/0.14</annotation></semantics></math> for EigenScape, which contain 570 minutes and 640 minutes of audio data, respectively.</p>\n474\t</div>\n475\t<div id=\"S3.SS1.p2\" class=\"ltx_para\">\n476\t<p id=\"S3.SS1.p2.1\" class=\"ltx_p\">Evaluation is done on three test sets.\n477\tFirst, we use a held-out AudibleLight set to test performance on unseen synthetic scenes.\n478\tSecond, we test on LOCATA tasks 1–4 <cite class=\"ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite\">[<a href=\"#bib.bib12\" title=\"The LOCATA challenge data corpus for acoustic source localization and tracking\" class=\"ltx_ref\">16</a>]</cite> (also high-resolution) and third, we test on STARSS23 <cite class=\"ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite\">[<a href=\"#bib.bib13\" title=\"STARSS23: an audio-visual dataset of spatial recordings of real scenes with spatiotemporal annotations of sound events\" class=\"ltx_ref\">19</a>]</cite> (only available as low-resolution tetrahedral format).</p>\n479\t</div>\n480\t<div id=\"S3.SS1.p3\" class=\"ltx_para\">\n481\t<p id=\"S3.SS1.p3.1\" class=\"ltx_p\">In summary, our experimental use of data is therefore modeled after Roman et al. <cite class=\"ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite\">[<a href=\"#bib.bib1\" title=\"Latent acoustic mapping for direction of arrival estimation: A self-supervised approach\" class=\"ltx_ref\">18</a>]</cite>, with two main differences. In addition to evaluating with LOCATA and STARSS23, we also evaluate using new AudibleLight test data. Also note that we completely exclude STARSS23 from model training.</p>\n482\t</div>\n483\t</section>\n484\t<section id=\"S3.SS2\" class=\"ltx_subsection\">\n485\t<h3 class=\"ltx_title ltx_title_subsection\">\n486\t<span class=\"ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection\">3.2 </span>Training</h3>\n487\t\n488\t<div id=\"S3.SS2.p1\" class=\"ltx_para\">\n489\t<p id=\"S3.SS2.p1.2\" class=\"ltx_p\">The upsampler is trained to reconstruct high-resolution 32-channel Eigenmike CSMs from a tetrahedral four-channel configuration, comprising Eigenmike microphones 6, 10, 22, and 26, consistent with previous literature <cite class=\"ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite\">[<a href=\"#bib.bib1\" title=\"Latent acoustic mapping for direction of arrival estimation: A self-supervised approach\" class=\"ltx_ref\">18</a>, <a href=\"#bib.bib2\" title=\"Robust DoA estimation from deep acoustic imaging\" class=\"ltx_ref\">17</a>, <a href=\"#bib.bib14\" title=\"Localization, detection and tracking of multiple moving sound sources with a convolutional recurrent neural network\" class=\"ltx_ref\">1</a>, <a href=\"#bib.bib6\" title=\"Sound source distance estimation in diverse and dynamic acoustic conditions\" class=\"ltx_ref\">15</a>]</cite>.\n490\tAs the reconstruction is performed independently for each frequency band <math id=\"S3.SS2.p1.1.m1\" class=\"ltx_Math\" alttext=\"k\" display=\"inline\" intent=\":literal\"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k</annotation></semantics></math>, the upsampler learns the mapping <math id=\"S3.SS2.p1.2.m2\" class=\"ltx_Math\" alttext=\"\\mathbf{C}^{(k)}_{\\text{low}}\\in\\mathbb{C}^{4\\times 4}\\rightarrow\\mathbf{C}^{(k)}_{\\text{high}}\\in\\mathbb{C}^{32\\times 32}\" display=\"inline\" intent=\":literal\"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐂</mi><mtext>low</mtext><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℂ</mi><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace=\"0.222em\" rspace=\"0.222em\">×</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">→</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐂</mi><mtext>high</mtext><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℂ</mi><mrow><mn>32</mn><mo lspace=\"0.222em\" rspace=\"0.222em\">×</mo><mn>32</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathbf{C}^{(k)}_{\\text{low}}\\in\\mathbb{C}^{4\\times 4}\\rightarrow\\mathbf{C}^{(k)}_{\\text{high}}\\in\\mathbb{C}^{32\\times 32}</annotation></semantics></math>.\n491\tThe CSM produced by the upsampler serves directly as the input to LAM.</p>\n492\t</div>\n493\t<div id=\"S3.SS2.p2\" class=\"ltx_para\">\n494\t<p id=\"S3.SS2.p2.1\" class=\"ltx_p\">We investigate three upsampler–LAM training strategies, illustrated in <a href=\"#S3.F1\" title=\"In 3.2 Training ‣ 3 Methodology ‣ MIND THE MICROPHONE GAP: BENCHMARKING ARRAY UPSAMPLING STRATEGIES FOR LATENT ACOUSTIC MAPPING\" class=\"ltx_ref\"><span class=\"ltx_text ltx_ref_tag\">Figure</span> <span class=\"ltx_text ltx_ref_tag\">1</span></a>.\n495\tIn the <span id=\"S3.SS2.p2.1.1\" class=\"ltx_text ltx_font_italic\">distinct</span> setup, the upsampler and LAM are trained independently and never interact during optimisation.\n496\tEach upsampler is trained with its own reconstruction loss;\n497\tThe trained upsampler is then paired with LAM in the form released by its original authors.\n498\tIn the <span id=\"S3.SS2.p2.1.2\" class=\"ltx_text ltx_font_italic\">aligned</span> setup, the upsampler trained in the <span id=\"S3.SS2.p2.1.3\" class=\"ltx_text ltx_font_italic\">distinct</span> stage is frozen, and only LAM is fine-tuned. The goal is to close the gap between upsampler and LAM.\n499\tThe mean squared error and total variation loss (MSETV) is computed on the LAM output only.\n500\tIn the <span id=\"S3.SS2.p2.1.4\" class=\"ltx_text ltx_font_italic\">end-to-end</span> setup, the upsampler and LAM are optimised jointly using the MSETV loss on the LAM output, with the upsampler remaining fully trainable throughout.</p>\n501\t</div>\n502\t<div id=\"S3.SS2.p3\" class=\"ltx_para\">\n503\t<p id=\"S3.SS2.p3.3\" class=\"ltx_p\">Across all experiments, the input audio, sampled at <span id=\"S3.SS2.p3.3.1\" class=\"ltx_ERROR undefined\">\\qty</span>24<span id=\"S3.SS2.p3.3.2\" class=\"ltx_ERROR undefined\">\\kilo</span>, is converted into a complex-valued CSM over nine linearly spaced frequency bands (<span id=\"S3.SS2.p3.3.3\" class=\"ltx_ERROR undefined\">\\qtyrange</span>1.54.5<span id=\"S3.SS2.p3.3.4\" class=\"ltx_ERROR undefined\">\\kilo</span>).\n504\tOptimisation uses AdamW (<math id=\"S3.SS2.p3.1.m1\" class=\"ltx_Math\" alttext=\"\\text{lr}=1.0\\times 10^{-4}\" display=\"inline\" intent=\":literal\"><semantics><mrow><mtext>lr</mtext><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1.0</mn><mo lspace=\"0.222em\" rspace=\"0.222em\">×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\text{lr}=1.0\\times 10^{-4}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id=\"S3.SS2.p3.2.m2\" class=\"ltx_Math\" alttext=\"\\text{weight decay}=1.0\\times 10^{-4}\" display=\"inline\" intent=\":literal\"><semantics><mrow><mtext>weight decay</mtext><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1.0</mn><mo lspace=\"0.222em\" rspace=\"0.222em\">×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\text{weight decay}=1.0\\times 10^{-4}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id=\"S3.SS2.p3.3.m3\" class=\"ltx_Math\" alttext=\"\\text{gradient clipping}=1.0\" display=\"inline\" intent=\":literal\"><semantics><mrow><mtext>gradient clipping</mtext><mo>=</mo><mn>1.0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\text{gradient clipping}=1.0</annotation></semantics></math>) with a batch size of 32, early stopping after 10 epochs without improvement on the validation loss, retaining only the best checkpoint.</p>\n505\t</div>\n506\t<figure id=\"S3.F1\" class=\"ltx_figure\"><img src=\"2607.24463v1/x1.png\" id=\"S3.F1.g1\" class=\"ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape\" width=\"830\" height=\"268\" alt=\"Refer to caption\">\n507\t<figcaption class=\"ltx_caption ltx_centering\"><span class=\"ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure\"><span id=\"S3.F1.2.1.1\" class=\"ltx_text ltx_font_bold\">Fig. 1</span>: </span>The three training strategies: rectangles are model components, red outlines indicate which are trainable while greyed-out ones have been trained previously, red arrows show the backpropagation loss.</figcaption>\n508\t</figure>\n509\t</section>\n510\t<section id=\"S3.SS3\" class=\"ltx_subsection\">\n511\t<h3 class=\"ltx_title ltx_title_subsection\">\n512\t<span class=\"ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection\">3.3 </span>Evaluation</h3>\n513\t\n514\t<div id=\"S3.SS3.p1\" class=\"ltx_para\">\n515\t<p id=\"S3.SS3.p1.1\" class=\"ltx_p\">We evaluate in terms of localisation performance (including both localisation error and recall), computational efficiency, memory requirements, and CSM reconstruction fidelity.\n516\tFor evaluation, all inference runs are carried out on an NVIDIA A100 40GB (training and inference are run on the same CUDA-enabled machine), a frame size of <span id=\"S3.SS3.p1.1.1\" class=\"ltx_ERROR undefined\">\\qty</span>100, 4 data loader workers, and an inference batch size of 1.</p>\n517\t</div>\n518\t<div id=\"S3.SS3.p2\" class=\"ltx_para\">\n519\t<p id=\"S3.SS3.p2.1\" class=\"ltx_p\">The predicted spatial maps are converted into direction-of-arrival (DoA) estimates using adaptive k-means clustering, in which the number of active sources is determined from the structure of the predicted intensity peaks, consistent with Roman et al. <cite class=\"ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite\">[<a href=\"#bib.bib1\" title=\"Latent acoustic mapping for direction of arrival estimation: A self-supervised approach\" class=\"ltx_ref\">18</a>, <a href=\"#bib.bib2\" title=\"Robust DoA estimation from deep acoustic imaging\" class=\"ltx_ref\">17</a>]</cite>.\n520\tThese DoA estimates are then evaluated using sound event localisation and detection (SELD) metrics.</p>\n521\t</div>\n522\t<div id=\"S3.SS3.p3\" class=\"ltx_para\">\n523\t<p id=\"S3.SS3.p3.1\" class=\"ltx_p\">Evaluation is performed in a class-agnostic setting, that is, source classes are ignored and all detections are treated as belonging to a single category.\n524\tK-means clusters with centroids within <math id=\"S3.SS3.p3.1.m1\" class=\"ltx_Math\" display=\"inline\" intent=\":literal\"><mrow><mn>10</mn><mo lspace=\"0em\" rspace=\"0em\">​</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">°</mi></mrow></math> of each other are merged. Others stay separated.</p>\n525\t</div>\n526\t<div id=\"S3.SS3.p4\" class=\"ltx_para\">\n527\t<p id=\"S3.SS3.p4.1\" class=\"ltx_p\">For latency and peak CUDA memory allocation measurements, we perform 10 warm-up runs followed by 10 measurement runs (all on LOCATA), and we report the median over these forward passes.\n528\tTo obtain the models’ computational cost, we measure the floating point operations (FLOPs) on the full input tensor.</p>\n529\t</div>\n530\t<div id=\"S3.SS3.p5\" class=\"ltx_para\">\n531\t<p id=\"S3.SS3.p5.1\" class=\"ltx_p\">To assess CSM fidelity throughout the model, we compute the Correlation Matrix Distance (CMD) <cite class=\"ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite\">[<a href=\"#bib.bib28\" title=\"Correlation matrix distance, a meaningful measure for evaluation of non-stationary MIMO channels\" class=\"ltx_ref\">7</a>]</cite> between the ground-truth 32-channel CSM and the CSMs produced at each stage: after the upsampler and after each of LAM’s denoising steps. CMD is based on the Frobenius norm and ranges from 0 (perfect correlation) to 1 (full orthogonality).</p>\n532\t</div>\n533\t<figure id=\"S3.T2\" class=\"ltx_table\">\n534\t<div id=\"S3.T2.61\" class=\"ltx_inline-block ltx_align_center ltx_transformed_outer\" style=\"width:433.6pt;height:440.7pt;vertical-align:-217.2pt;\"><span class=\"ltx_transformed_inner\" style=\"transform:translate(78.9pt,-80.2pt) scale(1.57196843663383,1.57196843663383) ;\">\n535\t<table id=\"S3.T2.61.61\" class=\"ltx_tabular ltx_align_middle\">\n536\t<tbody class=\"ltx_tbody\">\n537\t<tr id=\"S3.T2.2.2.2\" class=\"ltx_tr\">\n538\t<td id=\"S3.T2.2.2.2.3\" class=\"ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_t\" style=\"padding-left:3.0